THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
SRC_001 proposes adding a warrant-provenance monad that attaches a proof term to every accepted or rejected status during fixpoint iteration, but it does not describe the construction of the proof terms at all, and the claim that they are generated compositionally and are polynomial-size is unjustified. In an argumentation framework the status of a node depends on the status of its attackers, whose statuses in turn depend on their attackers, and so on; a proof term for a node at depth d naturally embeds the proof terms for its attackers at depth d+1. A compositional proof term therefore has size at least linear in the depth of the warrant DAG, and the number of distinct warrant paths can grow exponentially with depth, so the polynomial-size assertion is unsupported and likely false. This directly threatens the preserved guarantee of polynomial cost: even evaluating the status projection of a single node could require materializing an exponentially large proof term. Because the proposal does not give any concrete construction or size bound for the proof terms, it cannot be certified as preserving the polynomial-cost guarantee the problem requires.

TARGET:
Introduce a warrant-provenance monad into the fixpoint iteration: each accepted or rejected status carries a proof term in a DAG of warrants. The proof terms are generated compositionally and are polynomial-size. This turns the grounded extension into a proof-carrying artifact, enabling auditing of committed roots without altering the accepted set. The expansion preserves all guarantees because the status projection is unchanged. Criticizable: proof-term explosion in practice; the proof system must be shown sound and complete for the fixed point; 'validity of every committed root' becomes a proof-checking problem rather than a semantic guarantee.